  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 8.2. Increased capacity of member States to improve governance and sustainability of social protection systems</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">The National Health Insurance Scheme (NHIS) conducted an actuarial valuation and in December 2021, the board of NHIS adopted the report of the actuarial valuation. In April 2020, the Executive Secretary officially constituted a technical team led by the Director of Planning and Research Department of the Scheme that led the exercise. The actuarial valuation was carried out after consultations with relevant stakeholders including ILO&apos;s tripartite partners. The adoption of the report led to the revision of the service charge and drug tariff of the NHIS formal sector scheme and the improvement of administrative processes leading to higher quality of service to its clients. 
In December 2021, the government through the National Social Safety Net Coordinating Office (NASSCO) improved the management information system of the national social registry including  monitoring and evaluation. NASSCO developed a framework for data integration as basis for process flow of databases on poor and vulnerable households. This has resulted in increased coordination through interoperability with other databases within the social protection sector. Under the Joint SDG Project NGA/19/51/UND, the ILO engaged consultants to support the capacity strengthening of the NHIS technical team on the use of the ILO HEALTH MODEL for the actuarial valuation of the scheme. Eight technical training sessions were organised by the ILO between January 2021 to April 2021. ILO has supported the government agency (National Social Safety Net Coordinating Office) provided financial support to cover for staff costs of three project staff (Technical Advisor, Project Officer and Grant Management Officer) as per implementation agreement. In addition, ILO supported NASSCO through the engagement of a consultant to develop a process flow for defining linkages between the Unified Registry of Beneficiaries (URB) and Rapid Response Registry (RRR) database as a sub-set of the National Social Registry (NSR), developing harmonized checklists and synthesize the databases to establish interoperability and integration. ILO provided financial support for key activities of the project, such as National Social Registration handbook launch and dissemination event, training on gender and social inclusion and production of communication and visibility items.</narrative>
   </description>
